-- ---------- Blog ----------
  E'## Start with the reader, not with yourself\n\nMost resumes are written for the person writing them. The recruiter reading it is looking for a fast answer to three questions: does this person do the kind of work the role needs, at the level the role needs, in a way that suggests they will succeed here? Everything on the page should serve one of those questions.\n\n## Lead with the evidence\n\nPut your most relevant experience in the first third of the page. Use the language of the job description where it is honest to do so — a recruiter scanning for "Kafka" or "GST filing" should find it without hunting. Numbers help: team size, systems handled, budgets, volumes.\n\n## Cut what does not help\n\nObjectives, declarations, hobbies and long lists of every tool you have ever touched rarely move a decision. If a line would not change a recruiter\'s mind, remove it. Two focused pages beat four unfocused ones.\n\n## Make it easy to contact you\n\nPhone, email and city at the top. A working LinkedIn URL if you have one. Recruiters will call — make sure they can.'),
 ('contract-vs-permanent-hiring','Contract vs permanent hiring: which is right for your team?','Insights','Both models work — for different situations. A practical framework for choosing, with the cost and speed trade-offs employers usually miss.',array['Hiring','Contract staffing'],5,false,'PUBLISHED','2026-09-03','https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1553877522-43269d4ea984?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
  E'## Start with the shape of the work\n\nIf the work has a clear end date, a variable volume, or needs a skill you will not need again for a while, contract staffing usually wins. If the work is core, ongoing and benefits from institutional memory, hire permanently.\n\n## Count the real cost\n\nContract rates look higher per month but include payroll, compliance and replacement risk. Permanent hires cost less per month but carry recruitment time, notice periods and the cost of a wrong hire.\n\n## Speed matters more than people admit\n\nA contract engineer can start in days. A permanent senior hire takes 60–90 days including notice. If a project is waiting, that gap is the most expensive line on the sheet.'),
 ('questions-before-accepting-an-offer','The 5 questions to ask before accepting a job offer','Career Advice','Salary is only one line. Ask these five questions and you will avoid the most common regrets we hear from candidates.',array['Offers','Career growth'],4,false,'PUBLISHED','2026-08-27','https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
  E'## 1. What does success look like in six months?\n\nIf the manager cannot answer, the role is not defined yet.\n\n## 2. Who will I learn from?\n\nGrowth comes from people, not job titles.\n\n## 3. What happened to the last person in this role?\n\nPromoted, moved, or left — each tells you something.\n\n## 4. How are decisions made here?\n\nAsk for a recent example.\n\n## 5. What is not in the offer letter?\n\nHybrid days, review cycles, notice periods, bonus conditions. Get them in writing.'),
 ('hiring-cloud-devops-2026','Hiring for cloud and DevOps in 2026: what employers are looking for','Insights','Platform engineering, cost ownership and security-by-default have moved from nice-to-have to baseline. What that means for job descriptions and screening.',array['DevOps','Hiring trends'],7,false,'PUBLISHED','2026-08-20','https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
  E'## Platform, not just pipelines\n\nEmployers now expect DevOps engineers to build internal platforms that developers self-serve from, not just maintain CI.\n\n## Cost is a skill\n\nCandidates who can read a cloud bill and act on it are scarce and valued.\n\n## Security by default\n\nIAM hygiene, secrets management and supply-chain scanning are screening questions now, not bonuses.'),
 ('technical-interview-domain-switch','Preparing for a technical interview when you are switching domains','Career Advice','Moving from services to product, or from one industry to another? How to frame your experience so interviewers see transferable depth, not gaps.',array['Interviews'],5,false,'PUBLISHED','2026-08-13','https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1573497019940-1c28c88b4f3e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
  E'## Translate, do not apologise\n\nMap your past work to the new domain\'s vocabulary before the interview. Every industry has its own words for "throughput", "compliance" and "customer".\n\n## Bring one deep story\n\nOne problem you solved end to end beats ten bullet points.\n\n## Ask domain questions back\n\nCuriosity about the new domain is the strongest signal you will ramp fast.'),
 ('non-it-hiring-deserves-rigour','Why non-IT hiring deserves the same rigour as tech hiring','Insights','Plant engineers, accountants and sales teams carry as much business risk as developers. A case for structured screening beyond IT.',array['Non-IT','Screening'],4,false,'PUBLISHED','2026-08-06','https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581092160562-40aa08e78837?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
  E'## The cost of a wrong hire is not smaller\n\nA plant engineer who misses a tolerance or an accountant who misfiles GST creates real losses.\n\n## Structured screening works everywhere\n\nWork-sample questions, reference checks and clear scorecards apply to any role.\n\n## What we do differently\n\nWe screen non-IT candidates on the actual tasks of the job, not just tenure.');

